course: dictionary
lesson: 14025
title: Villains and strong words
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words are strong and a little dark. Writers use them to make a reader feel that something is wrong. «The malevolent witch cackled and cast a spell on the village.» Witches are not always evil, but this one wants to harm people. The word malevolent tells you so.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Cackled, cast a spell and village all point to trouble. If you swap in a kind word, the sentence feels wrong. Try it with nice or friendly and see how the story changes.
explain: Malevolent is about wanting to hurt others on purpose. «Its malevolent eyes glared at the children from the dark cave.» The eyes are not just unfriendly; they show that the creature wants to do harm.
fact: Malevolent is an adjective. It describes a person, creature or atmosphere.
fact: «Malevolent» means wanting to do harm or evil to others, and full of hate and spite.
fact: In stories, «malevolent» can also mean seeming evil and threatening, as in «a malevolent grin».
fact: The opposite of «malevolent» is «benevolent», which means kind and wanting to do good.
fact: «Malevolent» is a strong, slightly formal word, so it is good for creative writing.
exercise: «The malevolent witch cackled and cast a spell.» What does malevolent mean here? | Very tiny and quiet | Sleepy and tired after a long day | *Wanting to do harm and full of spite | Kind and wanting to help
exercise: Which word means the opposite of malevolent? | threatening | malicious | evil | *benevolent
exercise: «A malevolent grin spread across the villain's face.» What does the grin tell the reader? | The villain is very happy about a birthday | The villain is about to sneeze | *The villain wants to hurt someone | The villain has lost his hat
test: Which sentence shows a malevolent character? | «The sun rose slowly over the green hills.» | «They played football in the park all afternoon.» | *«He smiled cruelly and plotted to ruin the village.» | «She gave every child a warm hug and a biscuit.»
test: Why do writers use malevolent in stories? | To tell the reader the weather is sunny | To name a football team | *To show a character wants to hurt others on purpose | To describe a tasty cake
exam: Choose the best meaning of malevolent. | Sleepy after a long day | *Full of hate and spite, wanting to harm others | Very generous and kind to strangers | A bright shade of blue
